WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.607
This example creates a view with quality flags.

00:00:02.607 --> 00:00:06.637
It marks missing names, invalid email shapes, and invalid phone shapes.

00:00:06.637 --> 00:00:09.787
The final SELECT returns only rows that need attention.

00:00:09.787 --> 00:00:14.857
Because the source customer table is unchanged, the view becomes a transparent review layer.

00:00:14.857 --> 00:00:17.847
This is a strong pattern for reproducible cleaning.

00:00:17.847 --> 00:00:22.052
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:22.052 --> 00:00:27.357
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:27.357 --> 00:00:29.387
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:29.387 --> 00:00:35.227
In a real setting such as a healthcare pipeline validating contact, demographic, and event fields,

00:00:35.227 --> 00:00:40.612
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:40.612 --> 00:00:42.102
before discussing syntax.

00:00:42.102 --> 00:00:47.680
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
